> holomorphic sqrt(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  holomorphic except at sqrt (the branch cuts of the principal values)
> holomorphic log(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  holomorphic except at log (the branch cuts of the principal values)
> holomorphic exp(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  entire: holomorphic on the whole plane
> holomorphic z^(1/2)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  holomorphic except at non-integer powers (the branch cuts of the principal values)
> holomorphic conj(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
conj(z) is not holomorphic
  Cauchy-Riemann fails: at z = 1/2 + i/4, df/dx = 1 but df/(i dy) = -1
  (it uses conj, which is not holomorphic)
  Wirtinger derivatives there: df/dz = 0, df/dconj(z) = 1 (holomorphic means df/dconj(z) = 0)
> holomorphic 1/(z-1)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  holomorphic except at the points where it is undefined (poles)
> poles 1/(z^2+1)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z^2 + 1):
    z = -i  order 1, residue i/2
    z = i  order 1, residue -i/2
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> poles 1/(z^2-1)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z^2 - 1):
    z = -1  order 1, residue -1/2
    z = 1  order 1, residue 1/2
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> poles 1/((z-1)^2*(z+2))
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/((z - 1)^2*(z + 2)):
    z = -2  order 1, residue 1/9
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> poles 1/(z-1)^2
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z - 1)^2:
  none in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> poles 1/(z-1)^3
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z - 1)^3:
  none in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> poles 1/(z^2-1)^2
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
poles of 1/(z^2 - 1)^2:
  none in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7
  numerically: zeros of 1/f by Newton's method from a grid in |Re z|, |Im z| <= 7; orders from the growth of |f| on circles of radius 1e-2..1e-4, residues by circle integrals
> residue 1/(z^2+1) at i
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^2 + 1) at z = i:
  = -i/2
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^2 + 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/z at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/z at z = 0:
  = 1
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/z on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue log(z)/(z-1) at 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of log(z)/(z - 1) at z = 1:
  = 0
  log(z)/(z - 1) is regular there (or the singularity is removable): the residue is 0
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of log(z)/(z - 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue sqrt(z)/(z-1) at 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of sqrt(z)/(z - 1) at z = 1:
  = 1
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of sqrt(z)/(z - 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/((z-1)^2*(z+2)) at 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/((z - 1)^2*(z + 2)) at z = 1:
  = -0.1111111111
  a pole of order 2 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-2)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/((z - 1)^2*(z + 2)) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
